── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2604  overall: potential_risk
Spec: WRS.NTO allows indefinite hart stall in lower privilege modes unless M-mode firmware or hypervisor explicitly configures mstatus.TW/hstatus.VTW to enforce a bounded stall timeout with exception generation.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:135-154  (global definitions)
      MSTATUS_TW / SR_TW (bit 21, value 0x00200000) is entirely absent from csr.h. HSTATUS_VTW is defined at line 145, but no equivalent TW constant exists for mstatus/sstatus. The kernel cannot read or enforce the TW bit, which is the architectural mechanism that prevents unbounded WRS.NTO stalls from U-mode. The kernel exposes RISCV_HWPROBE_EXT_ZAWRS to userspace (sys_hwprobe.c:114) and uses WRS.NTO itself (cmpxchg.h:393) without ever verifying whether M-mode firmware has set the TW bit. On systems where M-mode firmware leaves TW clear — which is required for the kernel's own WRS.NTO usage — a malicious U-mode process can issue WRS.NTO and stall the hart indefinitely with no exception generated.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/sys_hwprobe.c:110-118  (hwprobe EXT_KEY array)
      Zawrs is exposed to userspace via hwprobe unconditionally when the CPU's ISA string includes 'zawrs'. There is no check of sstatus.TW (the S-mode-readable shadow of mstatus.TW) before advertising Zawrs availability. If TW=0 (as required for the kernel's own __cmpwait to use WRS.NTO), userspace also gains direct WRS.NTO execution capability. The kernel has no mechanism to selectively deny WRS.NTO to U-mode while retaining it for S-mode, but it could at least read sstatus.TW and refuse to expose Zawrs when TW=0, forcing userspace to use the safe PAUSE-based fallback.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/cmpxchg.h:367-441  __cmpwait
      The kernel's own WRS.NTO usage in __cmpwait is gated by CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_ZAWRS and a runtime riscv_has_extension_likely(RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZAWRS) check (line 376), falling back to ALT_RISCV_PAUSE() when Zawrs is unavailable. The comment at lines 360-365 explicitly acknowledges the bounded-termination assumption: WRS.NTO is expected to terminate within a reasonable time due to 'an implementation-specific other reason, a pending locally-enabled interrupt, or because it has been configured to raise an illegal instruction exception.' This is safe for in-kernel use where the kernel controls its own scheduling and interrupt state, but the same instruction exposed to U-mode without TW enforcement has no such guarantees.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/barrier.h:69-81  smp_cond_load_relaxed
      smp_cond_load_relaxed overrides the generic implementation to use __cmpwait_relaxed (and thus WRS.NTO) when CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_ZAWRS is enabled. This is kernel-internal usage only. The infinite loop (for(;;)) with WRS.NTO is bounded in practice because the kernel always has pending timer interrupts and the condition variable will eventually be updated by another hart.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:54-77  kvm_riscv_vcpu_context_reset
      HSTATUS_VTW is set unconditionally at line 74 during VCPU context reset, alongside SPVP and SPV. This ensures WRS.NTO from VS/VU mode always traps into HS-mode (KVM). The unconditional setting is the safe choice: VTW=1 even for guests that do not expose Zawrs, guaranteeing no guest can execute WRS.NTO directly. There is no path for userspace (QEMU/VMM) to clear VTW through the KVM ONE_REG interface — hstatus is not exposed as a settable register.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:83-88  wrs_insn
      The WRS.NTO emulation handler converts an unbounded hardware stall into a bounded kvm_vcpu_on_spin() yield operation. kvm_vcpu_on_spin() (in virt/kvm/kvm_main.c) attempts to yield to another runnable vCPU with a finite retry loop (3 attempts), then falls back to a directed yield or reschedule. The instruction pointer is then advanced past WRS via KVM_INSN_CONTINUE_NEXT_SEPC. This guarantees bounded execution regardless of whether interrupts or stores are pending.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:265-269  system_opcode_funcs (WRS entry)
      INSN_MASK_WRS (0xffffffff) and INSN_MATCH_WRS (0x00d00073) use a full 32-bit mask, matching only WRS.NTO (rs2=0). WRS.STO (0x01d00073, rs2=1) does not match and falls through to truly_illegal_insn(), raising an illegal instruction exception to the guest. If the guest is told Zawrs is available (via KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZAWRS), it may legitimately attempt WRS.STO and receive a spurious illegal instruction trap. This is a functional correctness issue rather than a DoS vulnerability — WRS.STO has an implementation-defined timeout making it inherently bounded — but it represents an incomplete Zawrs emulation.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #2604  overall: compliant
Spec: WRS.NTO allows indefinite hart stall in lower privilege modes unless M-mode firmware or hypervisor explicitly configures mstatus.TW/hstatus.VTW to enforce a bounded stall timeout with exception generation.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-155  switch_to_next_domain_context
      This function is a domain context switch that atomically swaps S-level CSR state (including sstatus, which carries mstatus.TW) between domains. It does not itself decide TW policy — it preserves whatever TW value was configured into the destination domain's saved context at initialization time. mstatus.TW enforcement is the responsibility of domain/hart initialization code, not the context-switch path. The function is structurally neutral with respect to the Zawrs/WRS.NTO spec.

